  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 239 AM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nThe frontal boundary will move very slowly south today with storms \nredeveloping along and east of the surface front for locations \ngenerally east of the Kansas turnpike this afternoon. Depending how \nmuch instability is realized ahead of the front there are enough \ningredients in places for a few strong to possible marginal severe \nstorm to materialize. This activity will move slowly east out of the \narea by around midnight. Meanwhile upslope flow over western Kansas \nlooks to spark off another round of storms later tonight with high-\nresolution models showing a possible forward propagating MCS diving \nsoutheast. Looks like this activity will remain west of \ncentral/south central Kansas. \n\nSurface high pressure will build over the area for tonight into \nMonday, and persist over the central plains through Wednesday. This \nwill give way to cooler below normal temperatures and dry weather \nconditions over the region. Models show decent low level moisture \nstreaming northward from the Texas panhandle into western Kansas on \nWednesday. This will result in numerous showers and storms \ndeveloping over western Kansas Kansas during the day on Wednesday \nwith some of this activity eventually spreading east into central \nKansas late Wednesday night into Thursday. Models continue to show a \nfavorable signal of deeper moisture streaming into Kansas for \nThursday and Friday with a upper level wave moving across the \nregion. This looks to bring decent chances of showers/storms to the \narea during this period. The cooler temperatures will continue into \nnext weekend.",
